  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If \\(x\\) varies as the cube of \\(y,\\) and \\(y\\) varies as the fifth root of \\(z,\\) then \\(x\\) varies as the \\(n\\)th power of \\(z,\\) where \\(n\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac1{15}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac53\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac35\\)",
    "D": "\\(15\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Translate each variation statement into an equation with a fixed nonzero proportionality constant:\n\\[\nx=Ay^3,\\qquad y=B\\sqrt[5]{z}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the second equation into the first shows how the two powers combine:\n\\[\nx=A\\left(B\\sqrt[5]{z}\\right)^3\n=AB^3\\left(\\sqrt[5]{z}\\right)^3\n=AB^3z^{3/5}.\n\\]\nHere \\(z^{3/5}\\) means \\((\\sqrt[5]{z})^3\\), which is real for every real \\(z\\), including zero and negative values. Since \\(AB^3\\) is constant, \\(x\\) varies as the \\(\\boxed{\\frac35}\\) power of \\(z\\), choice C.",
